course: dictionary
lesson: 5341
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. The sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer means. «He gave the ball a kick, and it flew over the fence.» Here kick is a hit with the foot, because the ball flew after it.
explain: Look for clues in the words near the word. «The match will kick off at three o'clock» means the match will start at three. Kick out can mean to throw someone out, and kick the habit means to stop a bad habit. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: Kick can also describe a feeling. «I get a kick out of it» means it is fun and exciting. Babies kick their legs, and horses can kick, so ask yourself who or what is doing the kicking. The subject of the sentence often gives you the meaning.
fact: Kick means to hit something with your foot, or to move your legs strongly.
fact: Kick can also be a noun: a free kick, a penalty kick or a corner kick in football.
fact: Kick off means to start a football match, or to start anything.
fact: «I get a kick out of it» is informal and means it is fun or exciting.
fact: Never kick or throw a ball at someone who is not ready, because it hurts and can start fights.
exercise: «The match will kick off at three o'clock.» What does kick off mean? | To hit the ball out of the ground | To leave the field | *To start | To finish with a loud shout
exercise: «At the cinema, do not kick the seat in front.» Why should you not do this? | *It could hurt or annoy the person sitting there | It makes the seats fly away | It is the only way to see the screen | It makes the seat more comfortable
exercise: In «She took a free kick near the goal», what kind of word is kick? | A question word | A colour | *A noun, meaning a hit with the foot | A verb meaning to sit down
test: «Mo gets a kick out of swimming.» What does get a kick out of mean? | *Enjoys it a lot, because it is fun | Stops doing it | Feels afraid of it | Pays money for it
test: «Stand to the side of the horse, not behind it, because it can kick.» Why? | Horses like to be stroked from behind | *A horse can kick you if you stand behind it | Horses only kick at night | Horses cannot see at the side
exam: In «The teacher will kick off the quiz at nine», what does kick off mean? | *To start the quiz | To fall asleep | To cancel the quiz | To finish the quiz
